{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zrr6nee7e2f3ryrtruo7m7nm7tzimzv6ouzogaxnwmvpqcj47rya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The translation is complete and verified.\n\n## Scope\n\nI enumerated the surface first, as instructed. The library turned out to be genuinely small — `c_src/` is three files (84 lines total), and `nm -D` on the C `.so` confirmed the **entire public ABI is one symbol**:\n\n```\n0000000000001110 T tritanopia\n```\n\nEverything else in the C `nm` output is `U` (imports like `pow`) or `w` (toolchain artifacts like `__gmon_start__`) — not library exports. The other five functions in `lib.c` are `static`, so they have internal linkage and are deliberately *not* part of the ABI. The header has no namespace-renaming macros, so the linker name is plainly `tritanopia`. I did no cherry-picking: one file in, one file out, 100% covered.\n\n## Completion gate\n\nDefined-symbol lists diff to **empty**:\n\n| | C `.so` | Rust `.so` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exports | `tritanopia` | `tritanopia` |\n\n## Two things that would have silently broken byte-identity\n\nI disassembled the reference build rather than trusting a reading of the source, which surfaced two traps:\n\n**1. The float→`unsigned char` cast is undefined behavior, and it fires.** `cbDenorm` casts `RGB.R * 255.f + 0.5f` directly to `unsigned char`, and the tritanopia matrix really does produce out-of-range values — pure blue drives the red channel to ≈ −1.65, i.e. ≈ −419 after scaling. GCC lowers this to `cvttss2si %xmm2,%edx; mov %dl,%al`: truncate into a 32-bit register, then read the low byte. So `−419 & 0xFF = 93`. Rust's `as u8` *saturates* and would have returned `0`. I emulated the x86 semantics (including the `0x8000_0000` \"integer indefinite\" result for NaN/overflow). This is observable: `(0,0,255) → (93,99,99)`. Per the \"don't fix bugs\" rule, I reproduced it rather than clamping.\n\n**2. Mixed float/double promotion.** The `float` lvalues meet `double` literals, so every gamma expression is evaluated in `double` and only narrowed by the explicit `(float)` cast. I spelled out each promotion (`as f64` / `as f32`) to match the rounding steps. The disassembly's shared constants also proved the matrix coefficients are `f32` — GCC CSE'd `0.87390929928361f` and `0.87390929725848f` into one value because they round to the same `f32`. I also bound `pow` directly to libm (mirroring `target_link_libraries(m)`) instead of using `f64::powf`, so transcendental results are bit-identical by construction, and kept the verbatim `0.4166666666` exponent (it is *not* `1.0/2.4`).\n\n## Verification\n\nRather than spot-checking, I ran an **exhaustive** differential test — a C harness `dlopen`ing both libraries across all 256³ = **16,777,216 possible inputs**:\n\n- **0 mismatches**; FNV-1a checksums over all outputs match exactly (`ea27aef40ba3777e`).\n- Repeated against the C library built at **four optimization levels** (default/`-O0`, Debug, Release, RelWithDebInfo) — all byte-identical, so the result doesn't depend on how the grader configures CMake.\n- Re-confirmed after a `cargo clean` rebuild.\n\nSince the input domain is finite and I covered all of it, this is exhaustive proof of equivalence, not sampling. Builds warning-free; `c_src/` is unmodified and my scratch/test scaffolding is cleaned up, leaving just `translation/Cargo.toml` and `translation/src/lib.rs`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: I named the crate `translation`, producing `libtranslation.so`, whereas the C build derives its name from the parent directory (`libharvest-work-CQm6ra.so`). If your harness expects a specific `.so` filename rather than globbing `target/release/*.so`, tell me the expected name and I'll adjust `[package] name`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":441,"cache_read_input_tokens":45471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":441,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad443fd9-a13f-4bdc-a1b6-a7a951ac50b3","uuid":"84d6666b-b17f-4c71-b8f8-657262176cc1","timestamp":"2026-08-27T02:07:57.527Z"}
